#
# The golden vector for the PurRDF entailment-regime string boundary
# (`purrdf_validate::regime`). ONE artifact, consumed by every host: the Rust
# test in `purrdf-validate`, and later the C-ABI and WASM crates, all call
# `purrdf_validate::regime::check_regime_golden_vectors()` over these bytes, so a
# host that diverges fails against the same expectations instead of against a
# fixture that quietly stopped agreeing with the other two.
#
# Format (see `parse_regime_vectors`): a line starting with `@` is a directive;
# every other line belongs to the body the last body-directive opened; outside a
# body only blank lines and `#` comments are legal. A body line may therefore
# never begin with `@` — neither N-Quads nor a rendered report ever does.
#
#   @case <name>      opens a case
#   @regime <name>    the regime's CLI spelling
#   @input            body: the N-Quads/N-Triples input document
#   @closure          body: the canonical N-Quads the closure must serialize to
#   @report           body: the report rendering the run must produce
#   @end              closes the case
#
# Every IRI is under `example.org`: PurRDF mints no vocabulary, and the only
# non-example.org IRIs below are the `rdf:`/`rdfs:`/`owl:` terms the entailment
# specifications themselves define.

@case simple-copies-a-named-graph-verbatim
@regime simple
@input
<http://example.org/A> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/B> .
<http://example.org/x> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/A> .
<http://example.org/x> <http://example.org/p> <http://example.org/y> <http://example.org/g> .
@closure
<http://example.org/A> <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#subClassOf> <http://example.org/B> .
<http://example.org/x> <http://example.org/p> <http://example.org/y> <http://example.org/g> .
<http://example.org/x> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://example.org/A> .
@report
purrdf-reasoning-report 1
regime simple
completeness exact
budget join-steps 0
budget stored-facts 0
budget term-arena-bytes 0
contract-hash 4151090ce6c2ecdae843e351420ddcf10f79e525c60b4c6d07bebeabaa07fbd5
inconsistency none
overclaims false
@end

@case rdf-types-every-predicate
@regime rdf
@input
<http://example.org/x> <http://example.org/p> <http://example.org/y> .
@closure
<http://example.org/p> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Property> .
<http://example.org/x> <http://example.org/p> <http://example.org/y> .
<http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#type> <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#Property> .
@report
purrdf-reasoning-report 1
regime rdf
completeness sound-incomplete 2
missing rdfD1
missing rdfD1a
fired rdfD2 2
boundary axiomatic-triples RDF and RDFS Semantics fix infinite sets of axiomatic triples — the container-membership family rdf:_1, rdf:_2, … above all — and no forward chase can materialize an infinite set; none is asserted, so rdfs1 and rdfs12, whose premises those axioms supply, have nothing to fire on
boundary datatype-value-space datatype value spaces are infinite, so the rules quantified over them (rdfD1 and rdfD1a for RDF and RDFS; dt-type2, dt-eq and dt-diff for OWL 2 RL) have no finite materialization; literals are compared by their lexical form and datatype IRI, never by their data value
budget join-steps 3
budget stored-facts 3
budget term-arena-bytes 168
contract-hash e3dfc92e2575713a6a555ef1dc5688d9086fe0a41c8eb0dd27aff5579db33158
inconsistency none
overclaims false
@end

